Proposed (14.1s): Use this skill whenever anyone worries that writing might come across as AI-generated, whether it's their own or someone else's. Examples: a writer who fears readers will think their newsletter, blog, essay, cover letter, or post came from a bot because of habits like heavy em dash use, words like "delve", or tidy three-part lists; a teacher, hiring manager, editor, or reviewer suspicious of a submission; someone who wants a draft checked for "ChatGPT voice" before publishing or sending; anyone asking to de-slop or humanize text. The skill flags passages that read as LLM output, explains why, says which habits are harmless, and revises without flattening voice. Use it even when the user names only one tell and asks what actually reads as machine-made. Also use it to check your own drafts. Skip it for plain proofreading, shortening, translating, punch-up edits where AI-sounding isn't the worry, and fiction.

